{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790264795,"nonce":"p7AWDCYuUYY2N85omrM56yNCkzs=","title":"Collector: clearing list slack costs about 5% on nqueens","message":"The slack invariant (b20d60ae3e42b6fce632a89f5182b162f64be3a6: the pool clears a block's last sixteen bytes on every allocation, list growth clears the capacity it gains beyond what the caller fills, pop/remove_at/clear/truncate clear what they vacate) costs nqueens (speed-center, -n 1, LLVM tier, NUC x86_64, quiet machine, 6 interleaved runs) wall median 6659 ms on main dea6d08b against 6970 ms (+4.7%); a build without the growth clearing ran 6836 ms (+2.7%), so growth clearing is about 2% and the rest (the per-allocation tail store, the stack clear before interpreter-to-compiled calls) about 2.7%. The speed harness showed 5680 against 5770 ms per iteration. nqueens reallocs a list buffer many times per place() call (set construction by push, the index table through resize_filled, the comprehension). Moving the growth clearing out of zyntax_realloc into list_reserve (clearing only past what the caller fills) did not measurably change it. Ideas not tried: a first growth sized to the final set size in the set builders so fewer reallocs happen; clearing freed blocks in bulk during the sweep only for classes that serve list buffers.","files":null}]}